<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet margin="0.5in" bottom="0.75in" xml:id="progress_check_1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Progress Check 1</title>
  <page>
          <exercise workspace="1.5in" xml:id="pc1_limit">
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
                                  An object is moving in such a way that its position <m>s</m> (in meters) at time <m>t</m> (in seconds) is given by the function  <m>s\left(t\right)=8{t}^{2}-10t+100</m>.  
                          </p>
                  </introduction>
                  <task xml:id="pc1_limit_setup">
                          <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
Set up, <em>but do not evaluate</em>, the limit
definition of the derivative that would find the derivative of the function at <m>t=1</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  \lim_{t\to{}1}\frac{s(t)-s(1)}{t-1}&amp;=
                                                                        \lim_{t\to{}1}\frac{8{t}^{2}-10t+100-98}{t-1}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Clearly explain why the limit you set up in <xref ref='pc1_limit_setup' text='type-hybrid'>Part</xref> will find the derivative of <m>s(t)</m> at <m>t=1</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                                  <solution>
                                          <p>
                                                  Using the two different ways we have defined the derivative, the limit to find <m>s'(1)</m>, the derivative of <m>s</m> at <m>t=1</m>, is
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                          s'(t) &amp;= \lim_{t\rightarrow 1}\frac{s(t)-s(1)}{t-1}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>\text{or}&amp;</mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                          s'(t) &amp;= \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{s(1+h)-s(1)}{h}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                          &amp;= \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\frac{s(1+h)-s(1)}{(1+h)-1}.
                                                          </mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                          </p>
                                          <p>
                                                          The expressions <m>\frac{s(t)-s(1)}{t-1}</m> for any value of <m>t</m> other than <m>1</m>, and <m>\frac{s(1+h)-s(1)}{(1+h)-1}</m>, for any non-zero value of <m>h</m>, are the average rate of change between the points <m> (1,s(1))</m> and <m> (t,s(t))</m>, or between <m> (1,s(1))</m> and <m> (1+h,s(1+h))</m>, respectively, so the closer <m>t</m> is to <m>1</m> or the closer <m>h</m> is to zero, the closer the average rate of change is to being an instantaneous rate of change.
                                         </p>
                                  </solution>
                          </task>
                  </task>
          <task workspace="2in">
                  <statement>
                          <p>
                                          Evaluate the limit you found in <xref ref='pc1_limit_setup' text='type-hybrid'>Part</xref>.
                          </p>
                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow> \lim_{t\to{}1}\frac{s(t)-s(1)}{t-1}
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{t\to{}1}\frac{8{t}^{2}-10t+100-98}{t-1}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{t\to{}1}\frac{8{t}^{2}-10t+2}{t-1}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{t\to{}1}\frac{(8{t}-2)(t-1)}{t-1}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{t\to{}1}(8{t}-2)
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=(8\cdot 1{t}-2)
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=6.
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          or,
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow> \lim_{h\to{}0}\frac{s(1+h)-s(1)}{t-1}
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{h\to{}0}\frac{8(1+h)^{2}-10(1+h)t+100-98}{h}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{h\to{}0}\frac{8{(1+h)}^{2}-10(1+h)+2}{h}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{h\to{}0}\frac{8{(1+2h+h^2)}-8-10h}{h}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{h\to{}0}\frac{8+16h+8h^2-8-10h}{h}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{h\to{}0}\frac{6h+8h^2}{h}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{h\to{}0}\frac{h(6+8h)}{h}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=\lim_{h\to{}0}(6+8h)
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow> 
                                                          &amp;=6.
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2in">
                  <statement>
                          <p>
                                          Find an equation for the tangent line to the graph of <m>s(t)</m> from <xref ref='pc1_limit' text='type-local'>Question</xref> at the point <m>t=1</m>.
                          </p>
                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          The tangent line goes through the point <m>(1,s(1))=(1,98)</m> and has slope <m>s'(1)=6</m>. Using the point slope form, an equation for the tangent line is
                                          <md>y=6(t-1)+98.</md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
          </task>
          </exercise>
  </page>
  <page>
          <exercise xml:id="pc1_interpret" workspace="0.5in">
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
A company's monthly revenue (money taken in) from car sales, <m>R</m> (in thousands of dollars), is a function of monthly advertising spending, <m>a</m> (in thousands of dollars). That is, for some function <m>f</m>, <m>R=f(a)</m>.
                          </p>
                  </introduction>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                          Interpret the following in practical terms, paying close attention to units. 
<md>
  f(80)=250.
</md>
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
<solution>
                                <p>
                                        When monthly advertising spending is <m>80</m><nbsp/>thousand dollars, monthly revenue is <m>250</m><nbsp/>thousand
                        dollars.
                                </p>
</solution>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
What does the company expect is true about the sign of <m>f'</m>? Why?
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
<solution>
        <p>
  The sign should be positive, because you expect more spending on advertising to bring in more
  revenue.
        </p>
</solution>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                        What does the statement <m> f'(100)=2 </m> mean in practical terms?
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                        <solution>
                                <p>
                                When monthly advertising spending is <m>100</m><nbsp/>thousand dollars, monthly revenue increases by <m>2</m><nbsp/>thousand dollars per additional thousand dollars spent on advertising.
                                </p>
                        </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  What does the statement <m>f'(100)=0.5</m> mean in practical terms?
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                                <solution>
                                        <p>
                                                When monthly advertising spending is <m>100</m><nbsp/> thousand dollars, monthly revenue increases by <m>0.5</m><nbsp/> thousand dollars per additional thousand dollars spent on advertising. That is, revenue increases by <m>500</m><nbsp/> dollars per additional thousand dollars spent on advertising.
                        </p>
                </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                                  <introduction>
                                          <p>
                                                  Suppose the company currently spends about $100,000 per month on advertising.
                                          </p>
                                  </introduction>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                  If <m>f'(100)=2</m>, should the company spend more or less than $100,000 on advertising?

                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                      If <m>f'(100)=2</m>, each $1,000 spent brings in an additional $2,000 in revenue, so additional
   spending makes sense.       
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                          If <m>f'(100)=0.5</m>, should the company spend more or less than $100,000 on advertising?
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
 <solution>
                  <p>
   If <m>f'(100)=0.5</m>, each $1,000 spent brings in only an additional $500 in revenue, which means
   they are spending more on advertising than they bring in, so additional advertising is not
   advisable. The company should spend less on advertising.
                  </p>
 </solution>
                          </task>
                  </task>
          </exercise>
  </page>
  <page>
          <exercise xml:id="pc1_cone_water_tank" workspace="1.0in">
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
                                  A water tank in the shape of a cone with the point upward with a base radius of <m>6</m><nbsp/>ft and a height of <m>24</m><nbsp/>ft, as shown in <xref ref="fig_wide_bot_cone" text="type-global"/>, is filling at a constant rate of <m>0.12~\text{ft}^{3}/\text{min}</m>. The goal of this problem is to construct a model for the depth of water as a function of time.
                          </p>
                          <figure xml:id="fig_wide_bot_cone">
                                  <caption>A Conical Tank with Base Radius 6<nbsp/>ft and Height 24<nbsp/>ft.
</caption>
                                  <image width="25%">
                                          <description>
                                                  <p>
                                                                  A conical tank, point upward, with base radius 6<nbsp/>ft and height 24<nbsp/>ft.
                                                  </p>
                                          </description>
                                          <xi:include href="./wide_bottom_cone.xml" />
                                  </image>
                          </figure>
                  </introduction>
                          <task workspace="0.5in">
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                  Find the total volume of the tank in cubic feet.
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          The total height is <m>24</m>ft and the radius of the base is <m>6</m>ft, so the total volume is given by
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          V_{T}=
                                                          &amp;=\frac{\pi}{3}R^{2}H
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{\pi}{3}(6\text{ft})^{2}(24\text{ft})
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{\pi}{3}36\cdot24 \text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=288\pi\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\approx{}904.7787\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                          Find the volume of water in the tank (in cubic feet) when the tank is filled to a depth of <m>2</m><nbsp/>ft and when the tank is filled to a depth of <m>4</m><nbsp/>ft.
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          When the tank is filled to a depth of <m>2\text{ft}</m>, the empty space above the water is a cone with height <m>(24-2)\text{ft}</m>, and the radius of its base is in the same proportion to the radius of the whole tank as <m>(24-2)\text{ft}</m> is to the full height of the tank. That is,
                                          <md>
                                                  \frac{r}{6\text{ft}}=\frac{(24-2)\text{ft}}{24\text{ft}}
                                          </md>
                                          so,
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  r&amp;=\frac{(24-2)\text{ft}{6\text{ft}}}{24\text{ft}}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  &amp;=\frac{(24-2)\text{ft}}{4}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  &amp;=\frac{22}{4}\text{ft}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          So the empty volume is
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          V_{\varnothing}
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{\pi}{3}\left(\frac{22}{4}\text{ft}\right)^{2}(22\text{ft})
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{\pi}{48}\left(22\text{ft}\right)^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{\pi}{6}\left(11\text{ft}\right)^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{1331\pi}{6}\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          and so, the volume of water is 
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          V_{w}&amp;=V_{T}-V_{\varnothing}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                               &amp;=\pi\left(288 -\frac{1331}{6}\right)\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                               &amp;=\frac{397\pi}{6}\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\approx{}207.8687\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                                  <p>
                                          When the tank is filled to a depth of <m>4\text{ft}</m>, the empty space above the water is a cone with height <m>(24-4)\text{ft}</m>, and the radius of its base is in the same proportion to the radius of the whole tank as <m>(24-4)\text{ft}</m> is to the full height of the tank. That is,
                                          <md>
                                                  \frac{r}{6\text{ft}}=\frac{(24-4)\text{ft}}{24\text{ft}}
                                          </md>
                                          so,
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  r&amp;=\frac{(24-4)\text{ft}{6\text{ft}}}{24\text{ft}}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  &amp;=\frac{(24-4)\text{ft}}{4}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  &amp;=5\text{ft}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          So the empty volume is
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          V_{\varnothing}
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{\pi}{3}\left(5\text{ft}\right)^{2}(20\text{ft})
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{\pi}{3}500\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{500\pi}{3}\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          and so, the volume of water is 
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          V_{w}&amp;=V_{T}-V_{\varnothing}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                               &amp;=\pi\left(288 -\frac{500}{3}\right)\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                               &amp;=\frac{364\pi}{3}\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\approx{}381.1799\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task workspace="1.5in">
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                          Find a formula for the volume of water in the tank (in cubic feet) when the tank is filled to a depth of <m>d</m><nbsp/>ft.
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                                  <hint>
                                          <p>
                                                          The volume of water is the volume of the whole tank minus the volume of the empty cone (from the surface of the water to the top of the tank).
                                          </p>
                                  </hint>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          When the tank is filled to a depth of <m>d\text{ft}</m>, the empty space above the water is a cone with height <m>(24-d)\text{ft}</m>, and the radius of its base is in the same proportion to the radius of the whole tank as <m>(24-d)\text{ft}</m> is to the full height of the tank. That is,
                                          <md>
                                                  \frac{r}{6\text{ft}}=\frac{(24-d)\text{ft}}{24\text{ft}}
                                          </md>
                                          so,
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  r&amp;=\frac{(24-d)\text{ft}{6\text{ft}}}{24\text{ft}}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  &amp;=\frac{24-d}{4}\text{ft}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          So the empty volume is
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          V_{\varnothing}
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{\pi}{3}\left(\frac{24-d}{4}\text{ft}\right)^{2}(24-d)\text{ft}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\pi\frac{(24-d)^{3}}{48}\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          and so, the volume of water is 
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          V_{w}&amp;=V_{T}-V_{\varnothing}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                               &amp;=\pi\left(288 -\frac{(24-d)^3}{48}\right)\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                               &amp;=\pi\left(\frac{13824-(24-d)^3}{48}\right)\text{ft}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                                  <p>
                                          
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task  workspace="0in">
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                  On the blank grid in <xref ref="pc1_blank_grid1" text="type-global"/>, sketch a graph of the depth of water <em>as a function of time</em>. Your sketch does not need to be perfect, but it should reflect how the rate of change of depth is changing as the tank fills.
                                                  </p>
                                                  <p>
                                                  You may assume the tank is empty to begin.
                                          </p>
                                  <figure xml:id="pc1_blank_grid1">
                                          <caption>A Blank Grid for Sketching the Graph of Depth as a Function of Time</caption>
                                          <image width="50%">
                                                  <description><p>A Blank Grid for Sketching the Graph of Depth as a Function of Time</p></description>
                                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pc1_blank_grid1">
                                        <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
                                        <coordinates bbox="(0,0,1200,50)">
                                                <grid />
                                        </coordinates>
                                        </diagram>
                                        </prefigure>
                                          </image>
                                  </figure>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                          <image width="50%">
                                                  <description><p>Depth as a Function of Time</p></description>
                                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pc1_depth_func_of_time_cone">
                                        <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
                                                <definition>f(x)=24-(13824-x*144/25/pi)^(1/3)</definition>
                                        <coordinates bbox="(0,0,7500,24)">
                                                <grid spacings="((0,625,7500),(0,2.4,24))"/>
                                                <graph function="f" stroke="black" />
                                        </coordinates>
                                        </diagram>
                                        </prefigure>
                                          </image>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                          Find an expression for the volume of water in the tank as a function of time, <m>t</m>, in minutes. (You may assume the tank is empty to begin.)
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          The volume of water as a function of time is given by <m>v(t)=(0.12t)\text{ft}^{3}</m>.                                   </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task workspace="1.5in">
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                  Equate the volume in terms of time with the volume in terms of depth and solve for depth. This yields a formula for the depth as a function of time.
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                         Equating volume as a function of time with the volume as a function of depth, we have
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  \pi\left(\frac{13824-(24-d)^3}{48}\right)\text{ft}^{3}
                                                          &amp;=0.12t
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          Solving this for <m>d</m>, we have
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  \pi\left(\frac{13824-(24-d)^3}{48}\right)=0.12t
                                                                  &amp;\longrightarrow
                                                          \left({13824-(24-d)^3}\right) =(0.12t)\frac{48}{\pi}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\longrightarrow
                                                          -(24-d)^3=1.8335t-13824
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\longrightarrow
                                                          (24-d)^3=13824-1.8335t
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\longrightarrow
                                                          24-d=\sqrt[3]{13824-1.8335t}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\longrightarrow
                                                          d=24-\sqrt[3]{13824-1.8335t}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
 
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <conclusion>
                                  <p>
                                  </p>
                          </conclusion>
          </exercise>
  </page>
  <page>
          <exercise xml:id="pc1_cylinder_water_tank" workspace="0.75in">
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
                                          A water tank in the shape of a horizontal cylinder with a radius of <m>3</m><nbsp/>m and a length of <m>8</m><nbsp/>m, as shown in <xref ref="fig_horiz_cyl" text="type-global"/>, is filling at a constant rate of <m>1.1\text{m}^{3}/\text{min}</m>. The goal of this problem is to approximate instantaneous rate of change numerically when the water is <m>2</m>m deep.
                          </p>
                          <figure xml:id="fig_horiz_cyl">
                                  <caption>A Cylindrical Tank with Radius 3<nbsp/>m and Length 8<nbsp/>m.
</caption>
                                  <image width="40%">
                                          <description>
                                                  <p>
                                  A Cylindrical Tank with Radius 3<nbsp/>m and Length 8<nbsp/>m.
                                                  </p>
                                          </description>
                                          <xi:include href="./cylindrical_tank.xml" />
                                  </image>
                          </figure>
                  </introduction>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                  Find the total volume of the tank in cubic meters.
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          V
                                                                  &amp;=\pi r^{2}h
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\pi (3\text{m})^{2}(8\text{m})
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=72\pi\text{m}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                          Find the volume of water in the tank (in cubic meters) when the tank is filled to a depth of <m>3</m><nbsp/>m.
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <m>V=\frac{1}{2}(72\pi\text{m}^{3})=36\pi\text{m}^{3}</m>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                          How long will it take for the tank to fill to a depth of <m>3</m><nbsp/>m if it starts empty?
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <m>\frac{36\pi\text{m}^{3}}{1.1\text{m}^{3}/\text{min}}\approx{}102.815\text{min} </m>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                  How long will it take for the tank to fill completely if it starts empty?
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <m>\frac{72\pi\text{m}^{3}}{1.1\text{m}^{3}/\text{min}}\approx{}205.632\text{min} </m>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task workspace="0in">
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                  On the blank grid in <xref ref="pc1_blank_grid2" text="type-global"/>, sketch a graph of the depth of water <em>as a function of time</em>. Your sketch does not need to be perfect, but it should reflect how the rate of change of depth is changing as the tank fills.
                                                  </p>
                                                  <p>
                                                  You may assume the tank is empty to begin.
                                          </p>
                                  <figure xml:id="pc1_blank_grid2">
                                          <caption>A Blank Grid for Sketching the Graph of Depth as a Function of Time</caption>
                                          <image width="50%">
                                                  <description><p>A Blank Grid for Sketching the Graph of Depth as a Function of Time</p></description>
                                                  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pc1_blank_grid2">
                                                  <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
                                                          <coordinates bbox="(0,0,1200,50)">
                                                                  <grid />
                                                          </coordinates>
                                                  </diagram>
                                                  </prefigure>
                                          </image>
                                  </figure>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                         <image width="50%">
                                                <description><p>Depth as a Function of Time</p></description>
                                                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pc1_depth_func_time_cyl">
                                                        <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
                                                                <definition>f(t)=(8*(t-3)*sqrt(9-(t-3)^2)+72*asin((t-3)/3)+36*pi,t)</definition>
                                                                <coordinates bbox="(0,0,72*pi,6)">
                                                                        <grid spacings="((0,6*pi,72*pi),(0,0.6,6))"/>
                                                                        <parametric-curve function="f" domain="(0,6)" />
                                                                </coordinates>
                                                        </diagram>
                                                </prefigure>
                                          </image>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                                  <introduction>
                                          <p>
                                                          The volume of water in the tank, in cubic meters, as a function of depth, <m>h</m>, in meters, is given by <md>V(h)=8\left(h-3\right)\sqrt{9-\left(h-3\right)^2}+72\arcsin\left(\frac{h-3}{3}\right)+36\pi</md>.
                                          </p>
                                  </introduction>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                          Use the volume function, <m>V(h)</m>, to find the volume of water in the tank at a depth of <m>1.9</m><nbsp/>m and at a depth of <m>2.1</m><nbsp/>m.
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          V(1.9)
                                                                  &amp;=8\left(1.9-3\right)\sqrt{9-\left(1.9-3\right)^2}+72\arcsin\left(\frac{1.9-3}{3}\right)+36\pi
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=8\left(-1.1\right)\sqrt{9-\left(-1.1\right)^2}+72\arcsin\left(\frac{-1.1}{3}\right)+36\pi
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\approx{}61.5055\text{m}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  V(2.1)&amp;=8\left(2.1-3\right)\sqrt{9-\left(2.1-3\right)^2}+72\arcsin\left(\frac{2.1-3}{3}\right)+36\pi
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=8\left(-0.9\right)\sqrt{9-\left(-0.9\right)^2}+72\arcsin\left(\frac{-0.9}{3}\right)+36\pi
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\approx{}70.5544\text{m}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                                  </task>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                          Use the fill rate to find how long it would take for the tank to fill from a depth of <m>1.9</m><nbsp/>m to a depth of <m>2.1</m><nbsp/>m.
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          The amount of water changed by <m>70.5544-61.5055=+9.0489\text{m}^{3}</m>, so it would take
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          \frac{9.0489\text{m}^{3}}{1.1\text{m}^{3}/\text{s}}
                                                                  &amp;=8.2263\text{s}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                                  </task>
                                  <task>
                                          <statement>
                                                  <p>
                                                          Calculate the average rate of change of depth as a function of time between the times when the depth is <m>1.9</m><nbsp/>m when the depth is <m>2.1</m><nbsp/>m.
                                                  </p>
                                                  <p>
                                                          Be careful! Your result should have units meters per minute!
                                                  </p>
                                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          \frac{\Delta{}h}{\Delta{}t}
                                                                  &amp;=\frac{2.1\text{m}-1.9\text{m}}{8.2263\text{s}}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\approx{}0.02431\text{m}/\text{s}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                                  </task>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                          Repeat the previous problem with depths of <m>1.99</m>
                                                                  and <m>2.01</m> meters.                                         </p>
                                  </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                   V(2.01)&amp;\approx{}66.4545\text{m}^{3}
                                                          &amp;
                                                   V(1.99)&amp;\approx{}65.5494\text{m}^{3}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          so the amount of time it takes to fill from <m>1.99\text{m}</m> to <m>2.01\text{m}</m> is
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          \frac{(66.4545-65.5494)\text{m}^{3}}{1.1\text{m}^{3}/\text{s}}
                                                          &amp;=\frac{0.9051\text{m}^{3}}{1.1\text{m}^{3}/\text{s}}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;\approx{}0.8228\text{s}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                          which means the rate at which the depth is rising is
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                  \frac{(2.01-1.99)\text{m}}{0.8228\text{s}}
                                                  &amp;=
                                                  \frac{0.02\text{m}}{0.8228\text{s}}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=0.0243\text{m}/\text{s}
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
          </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
